StreamDevice
------------
The databases in Db/ that talk to libcom1 devices (read_all_array, com1_get, the acknowledged
sets, identity and streaming in Db/libcom1.proto) use plain %g numbers and work with an
unmodified StreamDevice.

Only the legacy single query (com1_getLink, "q01...") needs the custom %z format. If you use
it, add StreamDevice.LibCom1ExponentConverter.cc when installing the StreamDevice support
module for EPICS (see file for specifics). No database in this repository uses it.

Serial or TCP
-------------
The protocol file is the same for both, only the asyn port in st.cmd differs:

  drvAsynSerialPortConfigure("AVR11","COM11")            # USB serial
  drvAsynIPPortConfigure("OPTA1","192.168.1.50:5025")    # TCP, see AVR/libcom1/examples/EthernetExample

Acknowledged sets
-----------------
The set records in Db/solenoidbox.db and Db/libcom1.db use com1_setLink_ack: the device answers
"ok", and a rejected or unanswered command puts the record into alarm. This needs libcom1
protocol 2 firmware. Older firmware answers ERR0 and does not execute the set, so flash a
device before loading the updated database for it. "ok" means the device accepted the command;
whether the hardware followed is still what the read-back PVs are for.

An alarm on a set record means "not confirmed", not "not executed". A device that was only
stalled finds the command in its receive buffer when it resumes and executes it then (seen in
the IOC test with a frozen board). After an alarm, check the read-back before setting again.

Polling, not streaming
----------------------
The databases poll the devices (read_all_array). Keep it that way on every port that has records
waiting for a reply (acknowledged sets, com1_get, identity). With streaming switched on ("P<ms>")
a frame can arrive between a command and its reply. StreamDevice then takes the frame for the
reply and the record goes into alarm although the device executed the command: 9 of 80
acknowledged sets at a 100 ms stream period in a test IOC. read_stream_array is for a port that
is only listened to.

Alarms
------
The element records (e.g. $(DEVICE):i1:get_interlock) read the array record $(DEVICE):data
through CA links with MS, so they turn INVALID when the device stops answering. Keep the MS
when adding records, otherwise a dead device looks like a healthy one showing its last values.

Db/libcom1_device.db adds identity records (device name, firmware, channel order) for any
libcom1 device with protocol 2 firmware.
